=== Order of the Objects on the Mat

The arrangement of the tools on the mat is not a mere act of material organization, but an extension of the discipline (Vinaya) and a reflection of the practitioner's mental clarity. Each object has a precise place, for external order is the mirror of internal order; a chaotic mat is, often, the sign of a distracted mind.

The strictly observed order is as follows: the spittoon, the tissues, and the water bottle. This sequence is not arbitrary, but responds to the functionality of the ritual and monastic hygiene. The spittoon must be placed so that it is accessible without interrupting the meditation posture, avoiding abrupt movements that break the state of recollection.

*The mat will be aligned at the front together with the spittoon and the asanas.*

This geometric alignment ensures that the sacred space of the hall maintains its visual harmony, eliminating any distraction for those meditating around.

*The bowls will be placed next to the spittoon, according to the old Thai tradition of rinsing the bowl before going out for pindapata.*

This act, although simple, reminds the monk of the importance of purity and preparation before interacting with the lay world, integrating physical cleanliness with spiritual disposition.

