=== Alternative Arrangement of the Monks' Seats

As can be appreciated in @zigzag, the monks' seats must be placed in alternating positions, respecting the order of ranks based on how many Vassas each one has.

While the zig-zag order is the preferred norm for its hierarchical clarity and spatial efficiency, there are exceptional circumstances that require an alternative arrangement. These variations should not be understood as a breach of discipline, but as a conscious adaptation to preserve the functionality of the hall and the respect due to the ranks.

In cases where the number of monks exceeds the capacity of the standard arrangement, or when the architecture of the hall imposes physical limitations, a linear alignment or compact blocks may be chosen. In such situations, the guiding principle remains unaltered: seniority (Vassa) must be the organizing axis. The most senior monk will always occupy the position of honor, and the rest will deploy following the decreasing order of their years in the monastic life.

This flexibility in arrangement allows the community (Sangha) to integrate harmoniously regardless of the number of participants. It is fundamental that any change in the structure of the seats be communicated and agreed upon previously, thus avoiding confusion during formal acts or the start of meditation.

The ultimate goal of any arrangement, whether standard or alternative, is to eliminate visual and mental noise. When each monk knows exactly where to be positioned and recognizes the place of the other, the physical space is transformed into a support for the practice, where the external structure sustains and facilitates internal stillness.

