== During the Meal

When we arrive at the hall after receiving the meal, we take our places in silence on our asanas. Ajahn Chandapalo will wait for the last member of the Sangha to sit before ringing the bell.

After the bell rings, eating begins in silence, without talking or looking at others. Of course, it is bad manners to start eating before the Abbot begins to eat. It is also poor etiquette to start eating before a monk of higher seniority than oneself.

Eating is an act of communion and gratitude, and must be received with respect and mindfulness. During the meal, it is recommended to maintain an upright posture and an attitude of absolute silence.

== After the Meal

In Vinaya we must use the Mop once more.

The period following the meal is a critical moment for the transition between physical sustenance and the resumption of meditative practice. In the Vinaya, discipline is not limited to formal acts, but extends to the management of the shared space. Therefore, it is a precept to use the Mop once more, ensuring that the hall recovers its purity and order immediately after use.

This act of cleaning should not be understood as a burden or a minor domestic task, but as a meditation in motion. By cleaning the hall, the monk practices mindfulness of each movement, transforming the action of sweeping into a process of purification of both the environment and the mind. Just as dust particles and impurities are removed from the floor, the conscious action seeks to remove the impurities of the heart, such as laziness (thina-middha) that often arises after food intake.

The restoration of order after the meal is also an act of generosity and respect toward the community (Sangha). By leaving the space impeccable, it is made easier for the next practitioner to find an environment of stillness and harmony, free from visual distractions. Rigor in post-prandial cleaning is, ultimately, a manifestation of gratitude toward the monastery and a reminder that the path to awakening requires constant vigilance, even in the simplest details of daily life.

== During the Night

The Buddha receives enlightenment 24/7.

Monastic practice does not stop with the setting of the sun; on the contrary, the silence of the night offers fertile ground for deeper introspection. It is fundamental to understand that the nature of Enlightenment is not subject to astronomical cycles nor to the alternation of light and shadow. The Buddha, as a symbol of absolute awakening, receives and emanates enlightenment twenty-four hours a day, reminding us that the light of wisdom (paññā) is an intrinsic quality of the liberated mind that shines with the same intensity at midnight as at midday.

In this sense, the maintenance of the hall during the night is not just a task of physical order, but an act of veneration. The space must remain in a state of purity and recollection, for the energy of the practice accumulated during the day remains impregnated in the atmosphere. The vigilance of the monk during the night hours is the extension of his meditation: a state of serene alertness where the emergence of dreams and the stillness of the body are observed.

The night is, therefore, the moment to consolidate what was learned during the waking hours. By maintaining discipline and respect for the sacred space even in the darkness, the practitioner recognizes that the path to awakening is continuous and uninterrupted. Enlightenment is not a punctual event, but a state of constant presence that transcends time and space.

